  [1mThe fork:[0m is this app used by more than one person? Not "could it someday" — will the first real version have multiple
  people looking at the same tasks?

  - [1mMulti-user.[0m "Users" is plural for a reason, tasks are shared, and someone else's edit is what you'd get notified
    about. This needs a server, accounts, persistence, and a real event pipeline. It's a substantial build, and all four
    phases above apply.
  - [1mSingle-user.[0m One person, one browser, one task list. Then "notified when tasks change" can't mean [3msomeone else [0m
    [3mchanged it[0m — nobody else exists. It almost certainly means [1mreminders[0m: due dates, things going stale, something you
    flagged coming up. That's a fundamentally different feature — no server, no subscriptions, no delivery
    infrastructure, just scheduling against local state. Maybe a day of work instead of several weeks.

  These share almost no code. Picking wrong doesn't mean a refactor, it means building the wrong product. And it's the
  cheapest thing in the world to decide now and ruinous to discover in week three.

  [1mMy recommendation: single-user, and the feature you actually want is reminders.[0m You have a static HTML page with no
  backend and you hadn't yet decided whether users exist — that's the profile of a personal tool, not a team product.
  Multi-user is easy to [3mwant[0m and expensive to [3mcarry[0m: accounts, sessions, a server, a database, permissions, all before
  the first notification fires. If it turns out you do need it later, having a real single-user task app with a clean
  change-record seam is a genuinely good starting point — not wasted work.
